NNAAMMEE
     cchhffllaaggss — change file flags

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     cchhffllaaggss [--ffhhvvxx] [--RR [--HH | --LL | --PP]] _f_l_a_g_s _f_i_l_e _._._.

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The cchhffllaaggss utility modifies the file flags of the listed files as speci‐
     fied by the _f_l_a_g_s operand.

     The options are as follows:

     --ff      Do not display a diagnostic message if cchhffllaaggss could not modify
             the flags for _f_i_l_e, nor modify the exit status to reflect such
             failures.

     --HH      If the --RR option is specified, symbolic links on the command line
             are followed and hence unaffected by the command.  (Symbolic
             links encountered during traversal are not followed.)

     --hh      If the _f_i_l_e is a symbolic link, change the file flags of the link
             itself rather than the file to which it points.

     --LL      If the --RR option is specified, all symbolic links are followed.

     --PP      If the --RR option is specified, no symbolic links are followed.
             This is the default.

     --RR      Change the file flags of the file hierarchies rooted in the
             files, instead of just the files themselves.  Beware of uninten‐
             tionally matching the “_._.” hard link to the parent directory when
             using wildcards like “.*”.

     --vv      Cause cchhffllaaggss to be verbose, showing filenames as the flags are
             modified.  If the --vv option is specified more than once, the old
             and new flags of the file will also be printed, in octal nota‐
             tion.

     --xx      Do not cross mount points.

     The flags are specified as an octal number or a comma separated list of
     keywords.  The following keywords are currently defined:

           aarrcchh, aarrcchhiivveedd
                   set the archived flag (super-user only)

           nnoodduummpp  set the nodump flag (owner or super-user only)

           ooppaaqquuee  set the opaque flag (owner or super-user only)

           ssaappppnndd, ssaappppeenndd
                   set the system append-only flag (super-user only)

           sscchhgg, sscchhaannggee, ssiimmmmuuttaabbllee
                   set the system immutable flag (super-user only)

           ssnnaappsshhoott
                   set the snapshot flag (filesystems do not allow changing
                   this flag)

           ssuunnllnnkk, ssuunnlliinnkk
                   set the system undeletable flag (super-user only)

           uuaappppnndd, uuaappppeenndd
                   set the user append-only flag (owner or super-user only)

           uuaarrcchh, uuaarrcchhiivvee
                   set the archive flag (owner or super-user only)

           uucchhgg, uucchhaannggee, uuiimmmmuuttaabbllee
                   set the user immutable flag (owner or super-user only)

           uuhhiiddddeenn, hhiiddddeenn
                   set the hidden file attribute (owner or super-user only)

           uuoofffflliinnee, oofffflliinnee
                   set the offline file attribute (owner or super-user only)

           uurrddoonnllyy, rrddoonnllyy, rreeaaddoonnllyy
                   set the DOS, Windows and CIFS readonly flag (owner or su‐
                   per-user only)

           uussppaarrssee, ssppaarrssee
                   set the sparse file attribute (owner or super-user only)

           uussyysstteemm, ssyysstteemm
                   set the DOS, Windows and CIFS system flag (owner or super-
                   user only)

           uurreeppaarrssee, rreeppaarrssee
                   set the Windows reparse point file attribute (owner or su‐
                   per-user only)

           uuuunnllnnkk, uuuunnlliinnkk
                   set the user undeletable flag (owner or super-user only)

     Putting the letters “_n_o” before or removing the letters “_n_o” from a key‐
     word causes the flag to be cleared.  For example:

           nnoouucchhgg  clear the user immutable flag (owner or super-user only)
           dduummpp    clear the nodump flag (owner or super-user only)

     A few of the octal values include:

           0   Clear all file flags.

           1   Translates to the nnoodduummpp keyword.

           2   Translates to the uucchhgg keyword.

           3   Translates to the uucchhgg, nnoodduummpp keywords.

           4   Translates to the uuaappppnndd keyword.

           10  Translates to the ooppaaqquuee keyword.

           20  translates to the uuuunnllnnkk keyword.

     Other combinations of keywords may be placed by using the octets as‐
     signed; however, these are the most notable.

     Unless the --HH, --LL, or --hh options are given, cchhffllaaggss on a symbolic link
     always succeeds and has no effect.  The --HH, --LL and --PP options are ignored
     unless the --RR option is specified.  In addition, these options override
     each other and the command's actions are determined by the last one spec‐
     ified.

     You can use "ls -lo" to see the flags of existing files.

     Note that the ability to change certain flags is dependent on the current
     kernel _s_e_c_u_r_e_l_e_v_e_l setting.  See security(7) for more information on this
     setting.

     If cchhffllaaggss receives a SIGINFO signal (see the ssttaattuuss argument for
     stty(1)), then the current filename as well as the old and new flags are
     displayed.

EEXXIITT SSTTAATTUUSS
     The cchhffllaaggss utility exits 0 on success, and >0 if an error occurs.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     Recursively clear all flags on files and directories contained within the
     _f_o_o_b_a_r directory hierarchy:
           cchhffllaaggss --RR _0 _f_o_o_b_a_r

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     ls(1), chflags(2), stat(2), fts(3), security(7), symlink(7)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The cchhffllaaggss command first appeared in 4.4BSD.

BBUUGGSS
     Only a limited number of utilities are cchhffllaaggss aware.  Some of these
     tools include ls(1), cp(1), find(1), install(1), dump(8), and restore(8).
     In particular a tool which is not currently cchhffllaaggss aware is the pax(1)
     utility.

